i'd like this quick take to adopt the  form  of what is called a pep talk now if  you're not familiar  with a pep talk because i think that's  kind of an american  idea it's a short talk that's supposed  to  get people feeling better to get them  kind of jazzed up to  help them to resolve to do  something and and the topic that i'm  going to talk about here  is one that i think a lot of people can  in fact use  a pep talk on especially when it comes  to  philosophy so a lot of my students  quite a few lifelong learners will  express  sentiments of feeling intimidated that's  the word that one person used  recently when approaching philosophical  works and this can extend more broadly  to  classic works of literature and all  sorts of other things  and the idea is there's this feeling  that comes with it that might  hold a person back from actually  engaging  with those works and we could use other  similar  feelings like fear or anxiety  a sense of perhaps even shame or guilt  or embarrassment that modality  of emotion a kind of reticence  that stands in the way of cracking open  the book  and starting on the path and adventure  of reading and this can indeed apply to  a lot of works it came up  in relation to the brothers karamazov  which is a very philosophical piece  of literature by theodore dostoevsky who  many people  consider to be you know one of the  greatest of russian writers so  you can see the bar is set pretty high  and that can promote a sense of you know  not feeling at  ease in going about studying these texts  and i i actually solicited  some you know ideas about what people  feel anxious or reticent or intimidated  about  i'm also drawing on my own experience  having taught students  for more than 20 years now and having  been a student myself  and so there's a number of common themes  that  come up and i'm just going to run  through them and you might find yourself  relating to these so people ask  themselves when they're approaching a  work of philosophy like you know  kant's critique of pure reason or rene  descartes meditations on first  philosophy  am i smart enough do do i have what it  takes or am i  not smart enough to be able to get  anything  out of the work and there's there's a  closely related  worry as well what if other people who  are clearly getting something out of  this work what if i can't  keep up with them what if i'm not having  the same kind of insights as they are  you know there's other worries about  these these works having  technical terminology what do all these  words mean  am i going to be able to wade through  them or  having a complex structure whether at  the level of the paragraph  or the chapter or the book itself  and you know there's also this worry oh  man these thoughts are really  deep am i be will i be able to get them  am i  the kind of person who can make sense  out of these things  there's also concerns about you know  maybe there's a whole background that i  need to know before i jump into these so  that i'll get something  out of it there's others  who express concerns like am i supposed  to understand this  all right away if i don't understand it  right away  then is there something wrong with me  how can i proceed if i don't understand  the first parts of what i'm reading do i  have to stay there until i  understand it before i move on to the  next  there's also concerns about not  understanding all these matters as  quickly as other students at least  appear to be  understanding it and you know there's  also  a worry maybe i'll understand things but  i'll actually understand it wrong i'll  get things  mixed up i'll misunderstand things and  i'll damage my  my mind or my progress pick whatever you  want these  these are all legitimate concerns in  some ways but in other ways  they're the sort of thing that i think  if you pick it and and you  question you'll find some of these going  away so if these are things that have  been hitting you  you know the first thing i want to say  is hey that's okay  a lot of people go through that  including myself i've  you know when i'm approaching something  that's way  out of my field philosophy is a very  vast  set of disciplines you know i might feel  some of these concerns myself but we  don't have to allow ourselves  to be you could say disabled or  guided by these one thing i do want to  say  is that i think a lot of people or at  least i suspect a lot of people  probably have these worries  weighing on them in part because of  their past experiences  and some of those past experiences could  have been you know reading a book and  not understanding it and feeling kind of  foolish or stupid or not as good as  other people as a result but i think a  lot of it comes from negative  experiences with  other people like teachers for example  saying what's wrong with you can't you  get this you know  and very often it's it's a sign of a bad  teacher  or you know having um social critics  or opinion formers tell us how wonderful  these books are particularly people from  the elites who had the opportunity to  study with private tutors  and you know have the confidence  building things in their life  allowing them to have that sort of sense  by the way  i'll tell you one thing elites don't  understand these books any better than  anybody else does  or it could be your own peers you know  particularly if you're  in an intellectual group there's  sometimes like jockeying for position  and people talking a lot of smack at  each other  you know i mean i i don't know how many  times i've seen people in  in various forums criticize each other  for not understanding hegel when none of  them under actually understand  hegel and they're just making each other  kind of feel bad and posturing sort of  like virgins who you know  talk about their sexual experiences that  they've never actually  had so you know if that's been the case  then you know  when you hear these voices arising  realize that they have an origin  and push them aside  the result of these these worries  can sometimes be that people won't read  texts at all  or they'll wait until the last minute  because they're so anxious about not  screwing it up and then they try to  blaze their way through the text  and it doesn't it doesn't come off well  so  you know sometimes people don't read the  text or they don't allow themselves  enough time to read them  or they do so and they feel crappy while  they're doing it because they're always  worried when's the next shoe going to  drop  something's going to happen i don't  understand and it takes away a lot of  joy  and it takes away a lot of excitement  and engagement that should be there for  you  as you're reading great works of  philosophical literature when you're  reading plato's symposium  or descartes meditations or mary  wollstonecraft's  vindication of the rights of women there  it's going to be difficult but there  should also be something in you  responding that that you know is a kind  of joy  and if that doesn't happen right away  that's okay it doesn't have to happen  right away that's another kind of  pressure you can take off your shoulders  so what can you do i'm going to give you  a couple of  pointers that might be useful for some  of you this is part of the pep talk  you know take the pressure off of  yourself you don't have to understand  everything right away you're okay you're  doing good  by putting yourself into it just like  the person who goes to the gym  you know they're not going to bench 450  right away or  press whatever they're going to do on  the leg press they have to work at it  and that's that's perfectly  normal going to the gym putting in the  time  that's the thing that really matters  if you find yourself engaging in  negative self-talk and  running yourself down talk back to that  say well how do you know that i don't  understand things  how do you know that i'm never going to  really make any sense of this  talk back to your inner voice because  your inner voice  it's not the the you know the oracle or  something like that  and you can also tell yourself that  it's okay that you do feel intimidated  or you know  anxious that's that's fine lots of  people feel that way  you don't have to allow that to control  you you  can read the works whether you  feel a certain way or not and your  feelings don't have to dominate you  remind yourself as well if you are  feeling these things that this is this  is natural and normal  it's just like approaching any other  thing you're going to learn an  instrument you're going to learn a  language  you're going to go into a very different  social setting you're gonna feel a  little bit  out of sorts but if you spend some time  there  and you get your you know get your  footing as we say you'll start to feel  better about it and you will be able to  learn something and understand something  and get something out of  the texts so the last thing that i want  to say and this is sort of the  ra ra pep thing is if you've got a work  a book that you have been leaving on the  shelf  and not opening up because you know  you're worried about  whether you can actually understand it  crack it open  what's the worst that can happen right  you start reading it  and it's you know eagles phenomenology  of spirit and you're confused  you're like everybody else and that's  okay just you know  keep working at it persevere keep coming  back to it  and you are capable of understanding  something nobody when it comes to these  great works of classic literature  nobody gets everything that's going on  the first time or  the tenth time or even the hundredth  time i don't know how many times  i've read through and even taught some  of these works and i find something new  quite often coming back to them myself  and i do so in conversation  with others where they're also surprised  as well so  take a little pressure off yourself and  go ahead and try  you are capable of studying philosophy  you
 

