thing i want to say  is hey that's okay  a lot of people go through that  including myself i've  you know when i'm approaching something  that's way  out of my field philosophy is a very  vast  set of disciplines you know i might feel  some of these concerns myself but we  don't have to allow ourselves  to be you could say disabled or  guided by these one thing i do want to  say  is that i think a lot of people or at  least i suspect a lot of people  probably have these worries  weighing on them in part because of  their past experiences  and some of those past experiences could  have been you know reading a book and  not understanding it and feeling kind of  foolish or stupid or not as good as  other people as a result but i think a  lot of it comes from negative  experiences with  other people like teachers for example  saying what's wrong with you can't you  get this you know  and very often it's it's a sign of a bad  teacher  or you know having um social critics  or opinion formers tell us how wonderful  these books are particularly people from  the elites who had the opportunity to  study with private tutors  and you know have the confidence  building things in their life  allowing them to have that sort of sense  by the way  i'll tell you one thing elites don't  understand these books any better than  anybody else does  or it could be your own peers you know  particularly if you're  in an intellectual group there's  sometimes like jockeying for position  and people talking a lot of smack at  each other  you know i mean i i don't know how many  times i've seen people in  in various forums criticize each other  for not understanding hegel when none of  them under actually understand  hegel and they're just making each other  kind of feel bad and posturing sort of  like virgins who you know  talk about their sexual experiences that  they've never actually  had so you know if that's been the case  then you know  when you hear these voices arising  realize that they have an origin  and push them aside  the result of these these worries  can sometimes be that people won't read  texts at all  or they'll wait until the last minute  because they're so anxious about not  screwing it up and then they try to  blaze their way through the text  and it doesn't it doesn't come off well  so  you know sometimes people don't read the  text or they don't allow themselves  enough time to read them  or they do so and they feel crappy while  they're doing it because they're always  worried when's the next shoe going to  drop  something's going to happen i don't  understand and it takes away a lot of  joy  and it takes away a lot of excitement  and engagement that should be there for  you  as you're reading great works of  philosophical literature when you're  reading plato's symposium  or descartes meditations or mary  wollstonecraft's  vindication of the rights of women there  it's going to be difficult but there  should also be something in you  responding that that you know is a kind  of joy  and if that doesn't happen right away  that's okay it doesn't have to happen  right away that's another kind of  pressure you can take off your shoulders  so what can you do i'm going to give you  a couple of  pointers that might be useful for some  of you this is part of the pep talk  you know take the pressure off of  yourself you don't have to understand  everything right away you're okay you're  doing good  by putting yourself into it just like  the person who goes to the gym  you know they're not going to bench 450  right away or  press whatever they're going to do on  the leg press they have to work at it  and that's that's perfectly  normal going to the gym putting in the  time  that's the thing that really matters  if you find yourself engaging in  negative self-talk and  running yourself down talk back to that  say well how do you know that i don't  understand things  how do you know that i'm never going to  really make any sense of this  talk